data: {"choices":[{"delta":{"reasoning_content":"Node 26 and npm 11. Now let me scaffold the Vite React app. I'll create the app manually to keep control over files, or use `npm create vite`. Let me use npm create vite with the react template in the current directory. Actually the directory has DESIGN.md in it, which is fine — vite create may complain about non-empty directory. Let me instead manually create the minimal files: package.json, vite.config.js, index.html, src/main.jsx, src/App.jsx. That's simpler and avoids scaffolding issues.\n\nActually, let me think about what the design brief requires:\n\n1. `npm install @codesweep-ai/ui` — takes latest version, let npm write version range into package.json, don't write \"latest\" myself.\n2. React with Vite, useState for state.\n3. Import styles at entry point: core.css, app-shell.css, button.css.\n4. Header and Footer belong to AppShell — wrap page in AppShell and place them inside.\n5. The catalog is at node_modules/@codesweep-ai/ui/catalog.json. Read props from dist/components/\u003cName\u003e.d.ts.\n6. Optional peers (mermaid, puppeteer, pixelmatch, pngjs) left out — meaning don't install them. npm may warn about unmet optional peers, that's fine. Actually \"optional peers\" won't be auto-installed... npm does install optional peer deps? npm v7+ installs peer dependencies by default, but optional peers (in peerDependenciesMeta with optional: true) are NOT installed by default. So leaving them out is automatic. Good.\n7. Dev server bind 0.0.0.0 port 5173.\n8. README.md with build command and serve command.\n9. .gitignore with node_modules/ and dist/ before first commit.\n\nLet me first create package.json manually with react, react-dom, vite, @vitejs/plugin-react. Then npm install, then npm install @codesweep-ai/ui.\n\nActually a cleaner approach: use `npm create vite@latest . -- --template react` — but it prompts when dir is non-empty. In npm 11 / create-vite recent versions, there's `--overwrite` maybe. Simpler to hand-write files. Vite React app minimal:\n\n- package.json\n- vite.config.js\n- index.html\n- src/main.jsx\n- src/App.jsx\n- src/index.css (maybe minimal for layout centering)\n\nLet me check what version of vite to use. I'll just use \"latest\"-ish by installing: `npm install react react-dom` and `npm install -D vite @vitejs/plugin-react`. npm will pick recent versions. Then `npm install @codesweep-ai/ui`.\n\nThen read catalog.json and the .d.ts files for AppShell, Header, Footer, Button to get props right.\n\nLet me do this. First write .gitignore (before first commit — well, the merge commit already happened, but that's the orchestrator's commit; \"your first commit\" means mine). Write .gitignore now.\n\nPlan:\n1. Write .gitignore.\n2. npm init -y, then edit package.json... Actually `npm install` will create package.json via npm init. Let me just write package.json by hand with proper fields, then run npm install to fetch deps. The brief says \"Install the design system with npm install @codesweep-ai/ui, which takes its latest version. Let npm write the version range into package.json\". So I should run `npm install @codesweep-ai/ui` as a command after package.json exists, letting npm add the dependency entry.\n\nSteps:\n- Write package.json (minimal, no deps).\n- Write .gitignore, vite.config.js, index.html, src/main.jsx, src/App.jsx, src/index.css.\n- Run `npm install react react-dom` and `npm install -D vite @vitejs/plugin-react`.\n- Run `npm install @codesweep-ai/ui`.\n- Read catalog.json and .d.ts files to check props (do this before finalizing App.jsx — I can read right after install, then adjust).\n- Build with npm run build.\n- Test dev server binding (maybe just verify config; could run vite dev briefly and curl).\n- README.md with build and serve commands.\n- Commit.\n- Reply.\n\nLet me first write the base files, then install deps, then read the component type definitions before finalizing App.jsx. Actually, I'll install first so I can read the .d.ts files, then write App.jsx with correct props. Let me write package.json, .gitignore, vite.config.js, index.html, src/main.jsx (style imports), and a placeholder App; then install; then read d.ts; then finalize.\n\nvite.config.js needs server host 0.0.0.0 port 5173:\n\n```js\nimport { defineConfig } from \"vite\";\nimport react from \"@vitejs/plugin-react\";\n\nexport default defineConfig({\n  plugins: [react()],\n  server: {\n    host: \"0.0.0.0\",\n    port: 5173,\n  },\n});\n```\n\npackage.json minimal:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"name\": \"hello-app\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"version\": \"0.1.0\",\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"dev\": \"vite\",\n    \"build\": \"vite build\",\n    \"serve\": \"vite preview\"\n  }\n}\n```\n\nHmm, \"serve command\" — the README should name the build command and the serve command. Serve could be `vite preview` (which serves the built app). I'll document `npm run build` and `npm run serve` (vite preview). Or maybe the dev server? \"serve command\" likely means how to serve/run the app. I'll document both: build = `npm run build`, serve = `npm run serve` (vite preview). And mention dev server too perhaps. Keep it focused: build command and serve command.\n\nLet me start. First create the files."},"finish_reason":null,"index":0,"raw_output":null}],"created":1793342920,"id":"chatcmpl-181e9f23","model":"accounts/fireworks/models/kimi-k3","object":"chat.completion.chunk","usage":null}

